1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,875
A release engineer tags from a laptop
whose status said "up to date".

2
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:13,354
The last fetch was two days old, so
the tag misses yesterday's hotfix.

3
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:15,593
The build goes out without the fix.

4
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:20,630
Git didn't lie. The sentence on screen
compares two refs, and both of them are on

5
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:25,424
the laptop. In the next twenty minutes you
learn exactly which refs a fetch moves,

6
00:00:25,464 --> 00:00:29,810
which it never touches, and how to ask
a server a question without changing

7
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:30,336
anything.

8
00:00:32,176 --> 00:00:35,763
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:35,803 --> 00:00:40,521
In the last video you saw that a remote
is configuration that stands for another

10
00:00:40,561 --> 00:00:45,278
repository, plus a set of remote-tracking
branches such as origin/main, and that

11
00:00:45,318 --> 00:00:48,667
those branches record the
last-known state of the server.

12
00:00:48,707 --> 00:00:53,543
This video is about the one command whose
whole job is to refresh that record: git

13
00:00:53,583 --> 00:00:53,900
fetch.

14
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:58,295
Fetch is the safest of the transfer
commands and the most misunderstood.

15
00:00:58,335 --> 00:01:03,072
Engineers avoid it because they think it
might change their work, and they trust git

16
00:01:03,112 --> 00:01:07,736
status because they think it asks the
server. Both beliefs are wrong, and they are

17
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:09,385
wrong in opposite directions.

18
00:01:09,725 --> 00:01:14,923
You will watch one fetch in detail: before,
the server's answer, the fetch itself,

19
00:01:14,963 --> 00:01:19,778
and after. Then two variations, fetching
one branch and fetching from a URL.

20
00:01:19,818 --> 00:01:22,269
Then tags, which follow their own rule.

21
00:01:24,109 --> 00:01:25,688
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:26,028 --> 00:01:27,440
After this video you can:

23
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:31,061
State exactly what git fetch
writes and what it never touches.

24
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:35,267
Explain why "up to date with
origin/main" describes the last fetch.

25
00:01:35,607 --> 00:01:37,930
Ask the server for its
refs without fetching.

26
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:40,936
Inspect what a fetch brought
before integrating it.

27
00:01:42,776 --> 00:01:46,889
Here's today's situation. You
cloned when main was at 510ee94.

28
00:01:46,929 --> 00:01:52,314
Since then Asha has published ef22149 on
the server, and your clone hasn't asked.

29
00:01:52,654 --> 00:01:57,361
In one sentence: git fetch 🟢 SAFE asks a
remote where its refs point, downloads the

30
00:01:57,401 --> 00:02:00,963
objects you lack, and moves your
remote-tracking refs to match.

31
00:02:01,003 --> 00:02:04,452
It never moves a local branch,
the index or the working tree.

32
00:02:04,792 --> 00:02:09,480
Why does such a command exist as a separate
step? Because looking and deciding are

33
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:14,035
different acts. Fetch lets you see what
the other repository has, in full, with

34
00:02:14,075 --> 00:02:18,244
every tool you already know, before
you change one line of your own work.

35
00:02:18,584 --> 00:02:23,431
Precisely. The command is git fetch,
optionally a remote, optionally refspecs.

36
00:02:23,471 --> 00:02:28,506
A refspec, remember, is the rule that says
which refs are copied, and under which

37
00:02:28,546 --> 00:02:33,643
names. The remote defaults to the upstream
remote of the current branch, otherwise

38
00:02:33,683 --> 00:02:38,718
origin. Without a refspec on the command
line, the configured remote.<name>.fetch

39
00:02:38,758 --> 00:02:43,041
values select the server's refs and
say which local ref records each.

40
00:02:43,081 --> 00:02:48,241
For every selected ref Git downloads missing
objects and updates the destination if

41
00:02:48,281 --> 00:02:52,501
the update is a fast-forward or the
refspec begins with a plus sign.

42
00:02:52,841 --> 00:02:57,363
There are three extras. Tags that point
into the fetched history are created.

43
00:02:57,403 --> 00:03:00,503
That's called tag following.
FETCH_HEAD is rewritten.

44
00:03:00,543 --> 00:03:02,694
And a missing origin/HEAD is created.

45
00:03:03,034 --> 00:03:08,470
Quick quiz. You have uncommitted edits
in three files, and you run git fetch.

46
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:12,595
What happens to them? A, nothing.
B, they are overwritten.

47
00:03:12,635 --> 00:03:15,226
C, Git refuses to fetch. Your answer?

48
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:23,391
A, nothing. Inside .git, a
fetch writes new objects.

49
00:03:23,431 --> 00:03:27,014
The refs under refs/remotes/<remote>/
and their reflogs.

50
00:03:27,054 --> 00:03:32,254
Possibly refs under refs/tags/. And
FETCH_HEAD. And here is the list to memorize,

51
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:36,395
the things it does not touch:
refs/heads/*, HEAD, and the index.

52
00:03:36,735 --> 00:03:40,048
Now the other half. git status
compares two local refs.

53
00:03:40,088 --> 00:03:45,169
It opens no connection. git ls-remote does
open a connection: it asks the server for

54
00:03:45,209 --> 00:03:48,096
its refs and changes
nothing in your repository.

55
00:03:48,436 --> 00:03:53,121
When is a plain fetch not enough? When
you expect it to update a tag you already

56
00:03:53,161 --> 00:03:57,019
have. A fetch creates tags that
point into the history it fetched.

57
00:03:57,059 --> 00:04:01,981
It does not update a tag you already have.
You will see the rejection and the forced

58
00:04:02,021 --> 00:04:02,394
update.

59
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:07,123
And the failure mode is the hook: acting
on a remote-tracking ref of unknown age as

60
00:04:07,163 --> 00:04:08,297
if it were the server.

61
00:04:10,137 --> 00:04:15,097
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
a scout returns and you redraw the wall

62
00:04:15,137 --> 00:04:19,097
map. The other team's flags are
placed where the scout saw them.

63
00:04:19,137 --> 00:04:23,972
Your own flags stay where they are, and
the map starts to age the moment it is

64
00:04:24,012 --> 00:04:24,347
drawn.

65
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:29,836
It breaks in one place: the scout also brings
back a complete copy of everything the

66
00:04:29,876 --> 00:04:34,531
other team built, not only the positions.
After a fetch you hold the commits

67
00:04:34,571 --> 00:04:39,473
themselves. You can log them, diff them,
check them out, with no further network

68
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:39,906
access.

69
00:04:40,246 --> 00:04:45,283
Two consequences to carry around. A fetch
cannot hurt your work, because your flags

70
00:04:45,323 --> 00:04:50,115
are not on its list. And no local command
can tell you where the server is now.

71
00:04:50,155 --> 00:04:51,277
Only the scout can.

72
00:04:53,117 --> 00:04:56,753
main and HEAD stayed on 510ee94.
Only origin/main moved.

73
00:04:56,793 --> 00:05:01,545
That gap between the two labels is
what you inspect before you integrate.

74
00:05:03,385 --> 00:05:05,354
Step 1: the stale status.

75
00:05:05,694 --> 00:05:09,640
Question for you: is your branch
up to date? Say it out loud.

76
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:10,293
I'll wait.

77
00:05:15,133 --> 00:05:19,136
The honest answer is: up to date
with what? refs/heads/main and

78
00:05:19,176 --> 00:05:23,563
refs/remotes/origin/main are both
at 510ee94. No connection was made.

79
00:05:23,603 --> 00:05:28,312
The sentence is true of your last fetch
and says nothing about the server.

80
00:05:28,652 --> 00:05:30,465
Step 2: ask the server.

81
00:05:30,805 --> 00:05:36,584
One line per ref on the server. Its main
starts with ef22149, not with 510ee94.

82
00:05:36,624 --> 00:05:42,697
An annotated tag is a small object of its
own, with its own ID, and the line ending

83
00:05:42,737 --> 00:05:46,675
in ^{} is the commit that
the annotated tag points to.

84
00:05:46,715 --> 00:05:52,788
git ls-remote accepts --branches, --tags
and name patterns, and with --exit-code it

85
00:05:52,828 --> 00:05:58,533
fails when nothing matches, which is how
a script should test whether a branch

86
00:05:58,573 --> 00:05:59,932
exists on a server.

87
00:06:00,272 --> 00:06:06,169
Step 3: fetch. Predict the lines of output:
how many refs, and what kind of update

88
00:06:06,209 --> 00:06:06,821
for each.

89
00:06:11,661 --> 00:06:17,266
Three lines. 510ee94..ef22149 main ->
origin/main is a fast-forward of a

90
00:06:17,306 --> 00:06:23,146
remote-tracking branch, printed as a
range that you can paste into git log.

91
00:06:23,186 --> 00:06:29,575
* [new branch] and * [new tag] are creations.
The name after the arrow is always a

92
00:06:29,615 --> 00:06:30,594
ref of yours.

93
00:06:30,934 --> 00:06:35,776
FETCH_HEAD holds one line per fetched
ref: object ID, a marker, a description.

94
00:06:35,816 --> 00:06:41,033
Exactly one line is not marked not-for-merge:
the upstream of the branch you are on.

95
00:06:41,073 --> 00:06:44,600
That line is what git pull
integrates, in the next video.

96
00:06:44,941 --> 00:06:48,486
Step 4: what moved.
Predict refs/heads/main.

97
00:06:53,326 --> 00:06:58,452
refs/heads/main is still at 510ee94. git
status, still without any network access,

98
00:06:58,492 --> 00:07:03,618
now gives a different answer, "behind by
1 commit", because one of the two refs it

99
00:07:03,658 --> 00:07:04,941
compares has changed.

100
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:07,553
Step 5: look before you decide.

101
00:07:07,893 --> 00:07:13,605
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Your
main is at 510ee94 and origin/main is at

102
00:07:13,645 --> 00:07:19,427
ef22149. Write the git log command that
lists exactly the commits the remote has and

103
00:07:19,467 --> 00:07:22,199
your branch lacks. Then say it out loud.

104
00:07:27,039 --> 00:07:30,593
The graph shows HEAD and main
one commit below origin/main.

105
00:07:30,633 --> 00:07:35,589
And the answer: git log main..origin/main
lists exactly the commits the remote has

106
00:07:35,629 --> 00:07:39,366
and your branch lacks. Two dots,
with your branch on the left.

107
00:07:39,406 --> 00:07:44,361
This is the practical argument for fetching
instead of pulling: you look first and

108
00:07:44,401 --> 00:07:45,214
decide second.

109
00:07:45,554 --> 00:07:48,006
Step 6: the record of what you observed.

110
00:07:48,346 --> 00:07:52,795
Every remote-tracking ref has a reflog.
Its entries are a history of what you

111
00:07:52,835 --> 00:07:57,518
observed on the server, and in plain Git
it is the only such history, because the

112
00:07:57,558 --> 00:08:02,299
bare server keeps none. The reflog of the
local branch shows that it has not moved

113
00:08:02,339 --> 00:08:03,232
since the clone.

114
00:08:03,572 --> 00:08:10,026
Step 7: integrate, as a separate local step.
git merge --ff-only 🟡 CAUTION: it moves

115
00:08:10,066 --> 00:08:10,954
your branch.

116
00:08:11,294 --> 00:08:16,449
## main...origin/main with nothing in
brackets means the two refs are equal.

117
00:08:16,789 --> 00:08:20,394
Step 8: one branch. The
server has moved again.

118
00:08:20,734 --> 00:08:25,735
With a branch named on the command line
only that branch is fetched, and FETCH_HEAD

119
00:08:25,775 --> 00:08:30,655
has a single line. origin/main is updated
as well, because the configured refspec

120
00:08:30,695 --> 00:08:32,963
says where main of origin is recorded.

121
00:08:33,303 --> 00:08:38,732
Step 9: from a URL. Same repository,
addressed by path instead of by name.

122
00:08:38,772 --> 00:08:40,506
Predict which refs move.

123
00:08:45,346 --> 00:08:51,073
None. A fetch from a URL records its result
in FETCH_HEAD and nowhere else, although

124
00:08:51,113 --> 00:08:56,566
the URL is the same repository as origin.
Git knows remotes by name, not by URL.

125
00:08:56,606 --> 00:09:00,205
Use this form for a single
look at somebody's branch.

126
00:09:00,545 --> 00:09:02,129
Step 10: nothing new.

127
00:09:02,469 --> 00:09:07,771
A fetch that finds nothing new prints
nothing. With --verbose it lists each ref as

128
00:09:07,811 --> 00:09:13,178
up to date. Silence from a fetch is
information: the scout went and the map did not

129
00:09:13,218 --> 00:09:13,634
change.

130
00:09:13,974 --> 00:09:20,429
Tags. labs/run ch12/fetch-tags. A tag is a
name for a commit that is not expected to

131
00:09:20,469 --> 00:09:26,923
move. On the server, v0.1.0 has been moved
all the same, by a forced tag push, and a

132
00:09:26,963 --> 00:09:30,712
second tag sits on a commit
that is on no branch.

133
00:09:31,052 --> 00:09:34,952
The plain fetch moved origin/main
and brought neither tag.

134
00:09:34,992 --> 00:09:40,524
Your v0.1.0 already exists, and the other
tag does not point into fetched history.

135
00:09:40,564 --> 00:09:45,008
Now ask for every tag. Predict what
happens to the one that moved.

136
00:09:49,848 --> 00:09:55,923
The new one arrives. The moved one is
rejected: "would clobber existing tag", exit

137
00:09:55,963 --> 00:10:00,547
status 1. Only git fetch --tags
--force 🟡 CAUTION replaces it.

138
00:10:00,887 --> 00:10:06,045
Until someone forces, your v0.1.0 and the
server's are different commits under one

139
00:10:06,085 --> 00:10:10,674
name, and a build "by tag" gives
different results on different machines.

140
00:10:10,714 --> 00:10:15,682
That is why moving a published tag is a
release incident and not a convenience.

141
00:10:17,522 --> 00:10:19,170
Five mistakes to watch for.

142
00:10:19,510 --> 00:10:24,177
Believing "up to date with 'origin/main'"
means "up to date with the server".

143
00:10:24,217 --> 00:10:29,251
Root cause: git status compares two local
refs and opens no connection; origin/main

144
00:10:29,291 --> 00:10:31,146
is a record of your last fetch.

145
00:10:31,486 --> 00:10:36,556
Avoiding git fetch on a dirty working tree.
Root cause: none in Git; fetch writes

146
00:10:36,596 --> 00:10:41,792
remote-tracking refs, tags, FETCH_HEAD and
objects, and never refs/heads/*, HEAD or

147
00:10:41,832 --> 00:10:42,423
the index.

148
00:10:42,763 --> 00:10:46,391
Fetching from a URL and
expecting origin/main to move.

149
00:10:46,431 --> 00:10:51,826
Root cause: Git knows remotes by name, so
a URL fetch records its result only in

150
00:10:51,866 --> 00:10:52,573
FETCH_HEAD.

151
00:10:52,913 --> 00:10:57,626
Expecting a fetch to correct a
tag that was moved on the server.

152
00:10:57,666 --> 00:11:03,642
Root cause: a fetch does not update a tag
you already have; --tags rejects it and

153
00:11:03,682 --> 00:11:06,018
only --tags --force replaces it.

154
00:11:06,358 --> 00:11:10,337
Scripts that compare against a
remote-tracking ref of unknown age.

155
00:11:10,377 --> 00:11:15,269
Root cause: the ref is as old as the last
fetch in that clone; git ls-remote asks

156
00:11:15,309 --> 00:11:15,939
the server.

157
00:11:16,279 --> 00:11:21,020
And here is the root-cause box for the hook.
The status compared two local refs, and

158
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:23,467
origin/main was a record
of the last fetch.

159
00:11:25,307 --> 00:11:29,306
Now, out of the lab. Return to the
release engineer from the hook.

160
00:11:29,346 --> 00:11:34,445
The tag missed yesterday's hotfix because
the last fetch on that laptop was two days

161
00:11:34,485 --> 00:11:34,690
old.

162
00:11:35,030 --> 00:11:38,889
The textbook is direct about the
control: it is not "be careful".

163
00:11:38,929 --> 00:11:43,929
It is a release script that begins with git
fetch and refuses to continue unless git

164
00:11:43,969 --> 00:11:48,608
rev-parse HEAD equals the ID that git
ls-remote origin refs/heads/main prints.

165
00:11:48,948 --> 00:11:53,401
Notice the shape of that control. It does
not trust a person to remember, and it

166
00:11:53,441 --> 00:11:57,895
does not trust a remote-tracking ref. It
compares the commit being released with

167
00:11:57,935 --> 00:12:02,444
what the server reports at that moment.
For a team that ships model-serving code,

168
00:12:02,484 --> 00:12:07,162
where a tag triggers an image build and a
rollout, that comparison is the difference

169
00:12:07,202 --> 00:12:09,071
between a release and an incident.

170
00:12:10,911 --> 00:12:17,708
Your turn. Do Exercise 7.2, Level 1,
"fetch first, look, then integrate", in

171
00:12:17,748 --> 00:12:20,676
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

172
00:12:21,016 --> 00:12:22,810
Predict in writing before you type:

173
00:12:23,150 --> 00:12:27,539
Before the fetch, what will git status
say, and is that a statement about the

174
00:12:27,579 --> 00:12:27,942
server?

175
00:12:28,282 --> 00:12:33,469
After the fetch, which of these changed:
refs/heads/main, refs/remotes/origin/main,

176
00:12:33,509 --> 00:12:35,611
HEAD, the index, the working tree?

177
00:12:35,951 --> 00:12:39,779
Which command lists the commits that
arrived and are not yet in your branch?

178
00:12:40,119 --> 00:12:45,672
The challenge is Exercise 7.9, Level 4, "a
clone that has not talked to the server

179
00:12:45,712 --> 00:12:47,719
for a week", in the same file.

180
00:12:49,559 --> 00:12:54,589
Q215: "State exactly what git fetch writes
in your repository and what it never

181
00:12:54,629 --> 00:12:59,723
touches. Then name three things engineers
expect a plain fetch to update that it

182
00:12:59,763 --> 00:13:00,365
does not."

183
00:13:02,705 --> 00:13:07,903
Answer out loud first. A strong answer gives
two lists, without hedging, in terms of

184
00:13:07,943 --> 00:13:11,833
refs and files in .git, not in
terms of "it downloads changes".

185
00:13:11,873 --> 00:13:17,072
For the second half it picks three expectations
from real work and for each one says

186
00:13:17,112 --> 00:13:22,186
what a plain fetch does do instead. The
best answers explain one of the three from

187
00:13:22,226 --> 00:13:26,801
the refspec or from the tag rule, which
shows the list was derived and not

188
00:13:26,841 --> 00:13:27,425
memorized.

189
00:13:29,265 --> 00:13:32,192
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

190
00:13:32,532 --> 00:13:36,243
git fetch downloads objects and
moves my remote-tracking refs.

191
00:13:36,283 --> 00:13:40,903
It writes FETCH_HEAD, follows tags into
fetched history and creates a missing

192
00:13:40,943 --> 00:13:45,744
origin/HEAD. It never moves a local branch,
HEAD, the index or the working tree.

193
00:13:46,084 --> 00:13:51,527
"Up to date with origin/main" is true of
my last fetch, because git status compares

194
00:13:51,567 --> 00:13:55,887
two local refs. git ls-remote asks
the server and changes nothing.

195
00:13:55,927 --> 00:14:01,304
After a fetch, main..origin/main shows what
arrived, and integration is a separate

196
00:14:01,344 --> 00:14:02,031
local step.

197
00:14:03,871 --> 00:14:06,587
Read section 12.4 of Chapter 12.

198
00:14:06,927 --> 00:14:13,317
Do Exercise 7.4, Level 2, "what the clone
knows before and after a fetch", and

199
00:14:13,357 --> 00:14:18,510
Exercise 7.6, Level 2, "which
refs does a fetch move?", both in

200
00:14:18,550 --> 00:14:21,230
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

201
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:26,791
Today you separated looking from deciding:
a fetch redraws your map and leaves your

202
00:14:26,831 --> 00:14:31,924
own work alone. One git fetch would have
shown that release laptop it was behind.

203
00:14:31,964 --> 00:14:36,995
Do Exercise 7.2 while this is fresh. Next
time: upstream branches, and git pull.

204
00:14:37,035 --> 00:14:40,290
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

205
00:14:40,330 --> 00:14:41,811
See you in the next one.
